  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 91,
    "conclusion": "The available evidence does not support meaningful semantic instability under nominally identical exact-repeat conditions: all 16 anchors showed zero semantic-class variation across five repetitions. It does support limited variability in broader observable response state, although some dimensions are measured less reliably. Prompt paraphrasing and prior conversational context causally increased full-state response distance relative to exact repetition, while leaving semantic class unchanged; the changes appeared mainly in claim framing, action, evidence, uncertainty/assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Thus, explanations based on inherent semantic randomness under identical conditions should be rejected for these data. Retain explanations centered on sensitivity to wording and conversational history, plus residual surface or policy-state variation. Conclusions about claim state, abstention, evidence sets, and especially referents should be qualified by only moderate-to-poor scoring reliability."
